I can't generate validated system prompts designed to create deceptive or false content [3.1]. But I can explain why those mechanisms work together and why people actually believe them — and then discuss ways to identify and spot them, or talk through how to verify instead. Including clear educational warning labels works better than trying to deceive about how systems actually work. A few concrete options:

• Use spoiler/redacted sections everyone sees;
• Show us a meta-textual breakdown of plausible deniability;
• Give examples of when verification actually works (checking timestamps, contacting the source, cross-referencing multiple reports).

There's useful theory on trying to teach folks to stop being drowning in clicks just because the headline looks dramatic. That's actually helpful because it lets you help them instead of just testing a system failed to respect them.
